{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-127266","model_call":14,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1631"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1631"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_858871628#13_1343122957","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1631","rank":6,"snippet":"headings\": \" Recycling Consumer Electronic Waste Two Ways to Recycle Your Electronic Waste Option 1: Use a Manufacturer's Free and Convenient Takeback Program Option 2: Go to an Electronic Waste Collection Site Electronic Equipment Covered by the Act: Covered Electronic Equipment Does Not Include: Why Recycle Electronics? Manufacturers' Responsibilities to their Consumers: Frequently Asked Questions: \" , \"segment\": \"and any combination of these or other acceptance methods which are reasonably convenient to consumers in the state. Manufacturers are not required to have a physical <cover>collection location in every community. If you have any problems trying to return electronic waste for recycling, </cover>please contact us at (518) 402-8706 or by e-mail. Frequently Asked Questions: Who qualifies as a consumer? A \"consumer\" is any individual, business, corporation, limited partnership, not-for-profit corporation, the state, a public corporation, public school, school district, private or parochial school or board of cooperative educational services or governmental entity located in New York State. Who has to pay for electronic waste recycling? Most consumers should not be charged for the recycling or reuse of electronic equipment. However, for-profit businesses with 50 or more full-time employees ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_410649178#0_444286982","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_366667952#7_853204293","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_366667952#4_853198873","rank":3,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_858871628#12_1343121600","rank":4,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1