{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-127266","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"informal\" \"e-waste\" recycling (+ health environmental) (+ lead mercury cadmium))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"informal\" \"e-waste\" recycling (+ health environmental) (+ lead mercury cadmium))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1276221077#1_2305275296","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1634","rank":1,"snippet":", \"headings\": \"The Human and Environmental Effects of E-Waste The Human and Environmental Effects of E-Waste References Date Authors Lucy McAllister \" , \"segment\": \"2 Developing countries with rapidly growing economies handle e-waste from developed countries, and from their own internal consumers. Currently, an estimated 70 percent of e-waste handled in India is from other nations, but the UNEP estimates that between 2007 and 2020, domestic television e-waste will double, computer <cover>e-waste will increase five times, and cell phones 18 times. The informal sector’s recycling practices magnify health risks. For example, primary and secondary exposure to toxic metals, such as lead, </cover>results mainly from open-air burning used to retrieve valuable components such as gold. Combustion from burning e-waste creates fine particulate matter, which is linked to pulmonary and cardiovascular disease. While the health implications of e-waste are difficult to isolate due to the informal working conditions, poverty, and poor sanitation, several studies in Guiyu, a city in southeastern China, offer insight. Guiyu is known as the largest e-waste recycling site in the world, and the city’s residents exhibit substantial digestive, "},"time":104},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":15,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
