{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-127266","model_call":33,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" (+ \"low recycling rate\" \"recycling rate\" \"only recycled\") (+ collection awareness challenge))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" (+ \"low recycling rate\" \"recycling rate\" \"only recycled\") (+ collection awareness challenge))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_417129682#12_1002533590","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1643","rank":1,"snippet":"minimum recycling targets based on their sales. The problem with a patchwork of laws is that no one state has enough market share to compel manufacturers to design greener or more durable products. By contrast, the European Union’s Restriction of Hazardous Substances Directive represents the entire EU market and thus has the clout to set higher standards for all electronic products sold in the EU. Its laws requiring manufacturers to help pay for recycling have resulted in an e-waste <cover>recycling rate of 35 percent, higher than that of the U.S. A federal law in the U.S. could help develop a more robust e-waste recycling infrastructure through setting targets and establishing financing schemes for collection </cover>systems and recycling plants. It could also offer a tax break or rebate to companies that process their used devices, and help prevent the export of e-waste to developing countries. However, a federal law is not likely to happen under this administration. A problem requiring multiple solutions With the flood of e-waste growing around the world, recycling alone will not be enough.\" , \"start_char\": 7230.000000 , \"title\": \""},"time":246},"model_calls":33,"document_judgments":28,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
