{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-127266","model_call":36,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" recycling (+ \"rare earth\" \"precious metals\" valuable) (+ challenges recovery separation))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" recycling (+ \"rare earth\" \"precious metals\" valuable) (+ challenges recovery separation))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_361607669#3_840144869","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1644","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_361607669#3_840144869\" , \"end_char\": 3255.000000 , \"headings\": \" Old Electronic Devices Provide Precious Metals for Recyclers The Importance of Electronics Recycling<cover>Recovery of Precious Metals Recycling of E-Waste</cover>\" , \"segment\": \"Recovery of Precious Metals The recovery of these precious metals is no small issue. While a modern recycling facility can recover as much as 95% of gold, in developing countries, the crude dismantling processes employed may recover only 50% of this precious metal. Also, if done incorrectly, the recovery process can expose workers to a wide range of hazardous substances. Overall, the current recovery rates of e-waste for processing are quite small. For example, for 2009 the U.S. EPA reported that only 8% of cell phones were recycled, along with 17% of televisions, and 38% of computers. Not enough of overall devices find their way to recyclers, and for the ones that do, not enough metals are recovered from those devices, on a global scale. Recycling results in only a 10 to 15% recovery of all the gold stored "},"time":295},"model_calls":36,"document_judgments":31,"relevance_3":15,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
