{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-127266","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1645"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1645"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_366667952#1_853192690","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1645","rank":5,"snippet":"\" , \"end_char\": 2362.000000 , \"headings\": \"Introduction to Electronics (E-Waste) Recycling Introduction to Electronics (E-Waste) Recycling Frequently Replaced Electronics What Happens to Devices at the End of Their Useful Life Benefits of <cover>E-Waste Recycling The Electronics Recycling Process Electronics Recycling Associations Current Challenges for Electronics Recycling Industry Electronics Recycling Laws Article Sources \" , \"segment\": \"What Happens to Devices at the End of Their Useful Life Unfortunately, the majority of these electronic products end up in landfills, and just 20% of e-waste is recycled. According to a UN study, about 50 million tons of e-waste was discarded worldwide. 2 Electronics are full of valuable materials, including copper, tin, iron, aluminum, fossil fuels, titanium, gold, and silver. Many of the materials used in making these electronic devices can be recovered, reused, and recycled—including plastics, </cover>metals, and glass. In a report, Apple revealed that it recovered 2,204 pounds of gold —worth $40 million—from recycled iPhones, Macs, and iPads in 2015. 3 Benefits of E-Waste Recycling Recycling e-waste enables us to recover various valuable metals and other materials ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_366667952#7_853204293","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":1},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
